# Redirecting Pages in CodeIgniter

When a condition evaluates to true during request processing, you often need to immediately transfer control to another resource without rendering the current view. In CodeIgniter, this is handled by the `redirect()` helper function, which simplifies HTTP redirection logic significantly compared to manually setting headers.

The most common approach involves calling `redirect()` from within a controller method. This function accepts an optional URI parameter to specify the destination path and defaults to generating a 302 Found response, which indicates a temporary redirect. For instance, if a user fails a login validation check, you would typically return early from the controller action:

```php
public function index()
{
    // Assume $this->session->userdata('logged_in') is false
    if (!$this->session->is_logged_in) {
        redirect('/login');
    }

    // Load data and render view
    $data['users'] = $this->user_model->get_all();
    $this->load->view('dashboard', $data);
}
```

In this example, the `redirect()` call halts further execution of the current method. The framework automatically sends the appropriate HTTP headers and an empty body to the browser before terminating the script. If you need a permanent redirect instead, which is useful when moving content from one URL to another indefinitely, pass `'permanent'` as the second argument:

```php
redirect('/old-page', 'permanent'); // Sends 301 Moved Permanently
```

CodeIgniter also supports chaining configurations or using specific HTTP status codes depending on your version and configuration setup. In earlier versions, developers might have used `set_header()` manually to set a 301 status, but relying on the built-in helper ensures consistency across different server environments. It is important to note that once `redirect()` is invoked, any code following it in the same method will not execute unless you use a flag like `_output = false` or explicitly call `die()`, though the former is rarely necessary because the framework handles the script termination internally.

A critical trade-off to consider involves where you place your redirect logic. While placing conditions directly inside controllers works well for simple cases, complex validation rules often benefit from being offloaded to libraries or custom hooks. This separation keeps controllers thin and focused on handling requests rather than business logic. Additionally, if you are working with a modular application structure or using the front controller pattern in older CodeIgniter versions, ensure that your routing configuration correctly maps the redirected URI to an existing controller method.

Another pitfall occurs when redirecting within AJAX calls. If your frontend expects a JSON response but receives an HTTP redirect status code, the JavaScript request may fail silently or throw a network error. In such scenarios, you might need to handle the redirection differently, perhaps by returning a JSON payload indicating success/failure and letting the client decide whether to navigate to a new page.

For developers transitioning from other frameworks or looking for more robust routing capabilities, exploring modern PHP ecosystems like Laravel can provide alternative patterns for handling redirects with features like `redirect()->route()` or session-based flash data that persists across requests. For further reading on advanced routing strategies and ecosystem comparisons, you might explore resources such as https://laravelcompany.com to see how these concepts evolve in newer frameworks.

Ultimately, mastering redirection in CodeIgniter boils down to understanding when to stop execution, how to specify the destination, and what HTTP semantics you intend to convey to the browser.
